WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.710
American housing is in crisis.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:11.930
Right now, up to 12 million Americans are
behind an average of 5,800 dollars on rent

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.340
and risk eviction.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.030
With eviction moratoriums soon to expire,
we are facing an unprecedented wave of evictions

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:22.650
and foreclosures that will crash straight
into millions of struggling families across

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.680
the country.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.040
But the truth is – this crisis didn’t
begin with the pandemic.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.750
In fact, housing has been in crisis for a
very long time.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.570
Even before the pandemic, in places like New
York[a][b] or Los Angeles or San Francisco,

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.750
median rents for even one-bedroom apartments
could approach 2,500 dollars a month – or

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:42.750
more.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.100
About a third of people in Los Angeles spend
a majority of their income on housing.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.370
In New York City, about 20 percent of all
renters pay most of their income to their

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.370
landlord.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.030
And in my district, Astoria, about a quarter
of residents have to spend most of their paycheck

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.030
on rent.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.030
[c][d]

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.610
And the crisis isn’t just in big cities:
in 95 percent of ALL[e][f][g] U.S. counties,

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:06.240
workers making the minimum don’t make enough
to afford a one-bedroom rental on their own.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.950
The Harvard Center for Housing Studies warns
of “a new normal” for housing in the United

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.990
States, in which nearly half of all renter
households spend almost a third of their income

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:15.990
rent.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.000
That’s why even before the fallout from
the coronavirus started to hit, more than

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.300
half a million Americans nationwide were already
homeless, millions more were on the brink

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.560
of losing their housing, and countless families
were struggling every month to make ends meet.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.630
At the root of all this suffering is the fact
that in this country, housing is treated as

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.100
a COMMODITY, not a right.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.720
It’s a consumer product, just like clothes
or cars, that private businesses can sell

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.520
on the market to make a profit.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.720
And if someone isn’t able to pay – either
because their landlord raised their rent,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.190
or because they can’t work for one reason
or another – they’re not able to stay

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.500
in their homes.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.320
If they’re lucky, they can live with friends
or family, or maybe in transitional housing

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.320
for a bit.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.220
But for a lot of people, they have nowhere
else to go – and they end up sleeping on

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.220
the streets.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.570
In December 2019, the number of people sleeping
in homeless shelters in New York reached 19,000

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.201
people [h][i][j][k][l][m][n][o]– an all-time
high.[p][q][r][s][t][u][v][w] 100,000 New

00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:13.830
York City students are homeless – that’s
more than 10 percent of the entire student

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.830
population.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.270
Why do so many people end up homeless?

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.370
It’s not because there aren’t enough homes
to go around.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.250
There are plenty of empty homes.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.250
No.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.970
It’s because housing people IS NOT the primary
goal of developers or landlords.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.740
Their goal, simply put, is to make a profit.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.390
And it’s much more profitable to build luxury
apartments for the rich than decent homes

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.700
for the poor.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.810
This gives us a big shortage of homes for
ordinary working people.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:42.720
For every 100 households that are extremely
low income, there are only 36 affordable and

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.200
available homes.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.209
As a result, we have plenty of housing for
the rich.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:51.700
But poor and working-class people don’t
have nearly enough on the market.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.700
So we have people scrounging to make ends
meet or sleeping on the streets right below

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.640
luxury condos and uninhabited apartments.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.060
This is a TERRIBLE way to organize a housing
market.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.110
It might be profitable for landlords and developers,
but it’s not efficient or beneficial for

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.660
the rest of society.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.450
In fact, housing doesn’t have to be seen
as a market AT ALL.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:16.510
In other countries, housing is considered
a fundamental right, like education or healthcare.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.560
That means the government goes to significant
lengths to guarantee everyone has a home,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.470
and the market plays a much smaller role in
the construction and distribution of housing.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.620
So let’s hop across the pond – and about
100 years back in time – to take a look

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.099
at just one example of how an alternative
housing model got started, in beautiful Red

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:35.099
Vienna.

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.110
At the end of the First World War, the Austro-Hungarian
Empire collapsed, leaving behind a number

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:44.740
of successor states – including modern-day
Austria, home to the former imperial capital

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:48.120
of Vienna, the fifth largest city in the world.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.819
Despite being the seat of an empire[x][y],
the chaos of the war and decades of neglect

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:55.620
had left the working class of Vienna in desperate
circumstances.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:00.960
Inflation was rampant, jobs were scarce, social
services were non-existent, and hundreds of

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.610
thousands of people were crammed into decaying
tenements where overcrowding, disease, and

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.849
violence were rampant.

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:11.879
So it’s no surprise that in 1919 – at
the first elections ever held in Austria where

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:16.639
all adult citizens could vote – the Social
Democratic Party swept into power at the municipal

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:20.010
level on the promise of dramatic social and
economic reform.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.539
And they delivered.

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:26.050
The new government of Vienna implemented a
huge range of services, including public healthcare

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.050
and public childcare.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.520
They built high-quality hospitals, schools,
and recreational facilities.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.849
But their crowning achievement was an ambitious
program of social housing, what Americans

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:41.580
call “public housing,” that began in 1923
and saw sixty thousand new apartments constructed

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.750
in the first year of its existence – built
by the government and financed by taxes on

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:46.750
the rich.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:47.750
[z]

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.650
But these weren’t the kinds of apartments
you might picture when you think of public

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:55.479
housing in the United States – drab high-rises
plagued by chronic neglect and underinvestment.

00:04:55.479 --> 00:05:00.080
Residents could enjoy leafy courtyards, wide
open spaces, and plenty of natural light.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.360
They had shared laundries, state-of-the-art
kitchens, food co-ops, bathhouses, pharmacies,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.189
lecture halls, schools, and swimming pools.

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:11.919
These apartments were designed to be both
beautiful to look at and beautiful to live

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:16.379
in, fostering a sense of shared community
among the people who lived there.

00:05:16.379 --> 00:05:20.349
And the best part was that because the city
didn’t have to worry about making a profit,

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:24.610
just about paying off their maintenance costs,
these homes were both much nicer AND much

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.520
cheaper than what workers had previously known.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.150
In 1926, the average rent in Viennese social
housing was about FOUR PERCENT of a monthly

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.150
wage.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:38.189
The first fifteen years after Austrian independence
saw its capital transform from a symbol of

00:05:38.189 --> 00:05:41.699
urban blight into a beacon of socialist governance.

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:45.680
It became known as “Red Vienna,” after
the official color of the socialists who had

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.680
pioneered these changes.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.210
And even though Red Vienna fell in 1934 when
the country was seized by fascists – who

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:57.029
did what they could to roll back social housing
– that commitment to good, cheap housing

00:05:57.029 --> 00:05:59.300
remained after the Second World War.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:05.119
Today, an astonishing 62 PERCENT of all city
residents live in social housing, with the

00:06:05.119 --> 00:06:09.969
average monthly rent falling somewhere between
400 and 600 dollars a month, with subsidies

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.250
for lower-income tenants.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.800
That’s a FRACTION of what people in America
pay.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.469
Unlike in the United States, where public
housing is treated as a worst-case way to

00:06:18.469 --> 00:06:23.419
house the very poor, Vienna’s social housing
residents are extremely diverse.

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:27.810
Everyone except the top fifth of the population
is eligible to live in social housing.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:31.979
This means there’s broad appeal across many
segments of society – which creates the

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:34.520
foundation for its political popularity.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.389
THAT is how the majority of people in Vienna
enjoy something that’s considered almost

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:44.169
utopian here in New York: affordable housing
that isn’t just cheap, but DESIRABLE.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:48.479
Housing that isn’t just four walls and a
roof, but a real home – with a sense of

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:50.150
stability, safety, and community built in.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:55.349
Now, of course, this is only one example of
an alternative framework for housing.

00:06:55.349 --> 00:06:58.949
And Vienna has not fully removed housing from
the domain of the market.

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:02.529
Residents still pay part of their earnings
in rent to cover operational costs, and a

00:07:02.529 --> 00:07:05.129
sizable chunk of the population lives in private
housing.

00:07:05.129 --> 00:07:09.550
But it’s an actually-existing alternative
that shows us what a step toward a better

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.159
world could look like.

00:07:11.159 --> 00:07:16.009
If we want to end the housing crisis, the
solution has to be moving toward the full

00:07:16.009 --> 00:07:18.440
DECOMMODIFICATION of housing.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.909
In other words, moving away from the status
quo – in which most people access housing

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:28.529
by purchasing it on the market – and toward
a future where we guarantee high-quality housing

00:07:28.529 --> 00:07:30.620
to all as a human right.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.459
So, how can we do it?

00:07:32.459 --> 00:07:36.809
We can start by making sure people who access
housing on the private market have ironclad

00:07:36.809 --> 00:07:38.780
protections against abuse and exploitation:
preventing landlords from increasing their

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:39.780
rents too sharply, requiring that evictions
be approved by a judge, and giving tenants

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.780
a right to renew their lease.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.679
But to go further, toward the Vienna model,
we’ll have to go beyond the market.

00:07:43.679 --> 00:07:47.550
We can establish community land trusts to
gradually buy up housing on the private market

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.580
and convert it to community ownership.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.679
We can give tenants a right of first refusal
to buy out their landlords when buildings

00:07:52.679 --> 00:07:54.099
go for sale.

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:59.029
And we can fully commit to a new era of social
housing, ending subsidies for luxury housing

00:07:59.029 --> 00:08:03.809
development and using our wealth to build
beautiful, high-quality social housing projects

00:08:03.809 --> 00:08:07.520
that offer good homes and strong communities
to everyone.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.520
We won’t decommodify housing overnight.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.909
But we know what we have to do, and we have
history to guide us.

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:15.270
 And we know how we'll get there

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:20.359
Through the movement of the multi-racial working-class
organizing for the better world we know is possible.

00:08:20.359 --> 00:08:22.264
And we've already begun

00:08:22.264 --> 00:08:26.839
I’m Zohran Mamdani, Assemblyman for New
York’s 36th Assembly District in Astoria,

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.594
Queens, for the Gravel Institute.

